const nlCrumpleSpot = {
  "prompt": "This crash-impact answer contains one faulty line — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "A 1,000 kg car at 20 m/s carries momentum p = m·v = 20,000 kg·m/s.",
    "A rigid wall stops it in 0.1 s, so F = Δp/Δt = 200,000 N.",
    "A crumple zone stretches the stop to 2 s, so F = 20,000 / 2 = 10,000 N.",
    "Lengthening the stop increases the force — that is why crumple zones hurt more.",
    "Same total momentum change, one twentieth of the force: the occupants barely notice."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Lengthening the stop DECREASES the force — F = Δp/Δt, and a bigger Δt buys a smaller F.",
  "why": "The momentum change is fixed; the force is its rate. Stretching 20,000 kg·m/s over 2 s instead of 0.1 s is what makes the stop twenty times gentler — the crumple zone is a time machine for the same Δp."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/foundational/newtons-laws">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Physics. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only the
    everyday experience of pushing things. Three sentences published in 1687 do the rest.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the three laws in plain words, and explain why the word "resultant" does the heavy lifting;</li>
    <li>distinguish mass from weight, and a balance from a spring scale;</li>
    <li>apply F = m·a to worked problems, and third-law pairs without cancelling them by mistake;</li>
    <li>compute moments of force and use the centre of gravity to predict stability;</li>
    <li>use momentum, impulse and conservation to explain crumple zones, airbags and rocket kicks.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: before any algebra, ask what the resultant force is — it alone decides how
    the motion changes.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1687 Isaac Newton published the <em>Principia</em> and, in three sentences, explained almost
    every push, pull and crash you will ever see. Before him, the physics of motion on Earth and
    the motion of planets were treated as separate subjects; after him, one set of laws governed
    both. The star of the story is the <strong>force</strong>: a push or a pull, measured in
    newtons (N), always acting in a particular direction — which makes force a vector, and means
    several forces on one object combine by adding with their signs and directions taken into
    account. What survives after that addition is the <strong>resultant force</strong>, and it is
    the resultant alone that decides how the motion changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three laws are worth stating in plain words before any algebra. <strong>First law:</strong>
    things keep doing what they are doing — sitting still, or moving in a straight line at steady
    speed — unless a resultant force intervenes. That stubbornness is called <em>inertia</em>, and
    it is why you lurch forward when a bus brakes: the bus stops, but you have no force on you
    (except the seatbelt, when it engages), so you keep going. <strong>Second law:</strong> the
    resultant force equals mass times acceleration. <strong>Third law:</strong> forces always come
    in equal-and-opposite pairs acting on <em>different</em> objects — you push the wall, the wall
    pushes back on you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below, the second law is plotted and then put to work as a calculator. Drag the force and mass
    sliders: the same push accelerates a truck far less than a bicycle. Double the mass and you
    halve the acceleration — that ratio <code>F/m</code> <em>is</em> the second law, everything
    else in this page hangs off it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="velocity grows in proportion to force and inversely to mass — the second law, plotted" widget="plotter" config={vtCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the gradient of the line is F/m — the second law drawn
    as a graph. The same push (same F) draws a steeper line for the bicycle than for the
    truck; double the mass and the gradient halves. Constant force gives a straight line:
    velocity grows in proportion to time.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the second law as a calculator — plus what your mass weighs on Earth" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the readouts separate the second law's jobs. The
    acceleration readout is F/m used as a calculator; the "force needed for 5 m/s²" readout
    runs the law backwards — scale the force to the acceleration you want — and the weight
    readout (m·g) is a different formula for a different idea, which is exactly the
    mass-versus-weight distinction the next section takes up.
  </p>

  <h2>Mass and weight: not the same thing</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mass</strong> is the amount of matter in an object, measured in kilograms. It is a
    scalar, it does not depend on location, and it plays a second role as well: mass is the measure
    of inertia, the resistance to being accelerated. A loaded supermarket trolley is hard to start
    moving and hard to stop for exactly the same reason — its mass. <strong>Weight</strong> is
    something completely different: the gravitational <em>force</em> acting on that mass, measured
    in newtons, pointing vertically down, and given by <code>W = m·g</code>, where g ≈ 9.8 N/kg
    near the Earth's surface. Mass lives in the object; weight lives in the interaction between
    the object and the planet.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file the quantities — mass or weight?" widget="sort-buckets" config={nlMassBuckets} />
  <p>
    The distinction is easiest to see off-Earth. A 60 kg astronaut has a weight of about
    60 × 9.8 ≈ 590 N on Earth, but on the Moon, where surface gravity is about 1.6 N/kg, the same
    astronaut weighs only about 96 N — while her mass is 60 kg in both places, which is why she
    finds it just as hard to get moving (or stop) inside the lunar module. It also explains the two
    measuring instruments: a <em>balance</em> compares masses against known standards and reads the
    same on the Moon; a <em>spring scale</em> measures the pull of gravity and would read a sixth
    of its Earth value there.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pin the Moon's pull on the gravity scale" widget="place" config={nlMoonPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> astronauts float not because there is no gravity up there but
  because they are in free fall. At the International Space Station's altitude of about 400 km,
  Earth's gravity is still roughly 89% of its surface strength; the station and everyone in it are
  simply falling around the planet together, so nothing pushes up on the astronauts and they feel
  weightless. We return to this in "Going deeper".</p>

  <h2>The first law: inertia</h2>
  <p>
    Formally: an object stays at rest, or keeps moving in a straight line at constant velocity,
    unless acted on by a resultant force. The subtle word is <em>resultant</em>. A car cruising at
    a steady 90 km/h has plenty of forces on it — engine thrust forward, drag and friction
    backward, weight down, road reaction up — but they cancel, so the velocity never changes. This
    law was revolutionary because it reversed two thousand years of Aristotelian intuition. Objects
    slow down in daily life not because motion naturally decays, but because friction and drag —
    invisible but ubiquitous — are always removing momentum. Remove the friction, as an air-table
    does for a sliding puck, and motion goes on and on, needing no force at all to sustain it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Engineering takes inertia seriously everywhere things accelerate. Headrests exist because in a
    rear-end collision the car (and your torso, pushed by the seat) accelerates forward while your
    head, with no force on it yet, momentarily stays put — whiplash is the first law acting on your
    neck. Seatbelts, luggage straps in overhead racks and the instruction to stand facing the
    direction of travel on a bus are all the same law, politely anticipated.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="bet first — why do you lurch when the bus brakes?" widget="predict" config={nlBusPredict} />

  <h2>The second law: force = mass × acceleration</h2>
  <p>
    The second law quantifies the first. In symbols: <code>F = m·a</code>, where F is the
    <em>resultant</em> force in newtons, m the mass in kilograms, a the acceleration in m/s². The
    equation even defines the unit: 1 newton is the force that accelerates 1 kg at 1 m/s². Notice
    what the law does not say — it does not say force causes velocity. Force causes
    <em>change</em> in velocity. Steady motion needs no force; <em>changing</em> motion always
    needs one.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example with real numbers: a 1,200 kg car has engine thrust of 3,000 N and total
    resistance (drag plus friction) of 600 N. The resultant is 3,000 − 600 = 2,400 N forward, so
    the acceleration is 2,400 ÷ 1,200 = 2 m/s². If the driver now lifts off, thrust vanishes and
    the resultant becomes 600 N backward: deceleration of 0.5 m/s². Every dynamics problem you
    will meet has this shape: draw the forces, find the resultant along the direction of motion,
    divide by the mass.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the force of your hand pushing a box across the floor is not
  what "keeps it moving". Once the box glides at constant velocity, your push exactly balances
  friction; the <em>resultant</em> is zero and the second law obediently gives zero acceleration.
  The push is needed only because friction never sleeps.</p>

  <h2>The third law: pairs, not cancellation</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Third law:</strong> if object A pushes object B with force F, then B pushes A with
    an equal force in the opposite direction. The critical phrase is "on different objects". When
    you fire a rocket, the engine hurls exhaust gases backward and the gases push the rocket
    forward — that is how rockets work in the vacuum of space, where there is nothing to push
    against. When you swim, you shove water backward and the water shoves you forward. When you
    walk, your shoe grips the pavement and pushes it backward; the pavement pushes you forward. The
    two forces of a pair never cancel each other's effects, because they act on different bodies
    with different masses — which is why the Earth, kicked backward by every footstep, accelerates
    an immeasurably tiny amount while you accelerate by a useful one.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each force with its genuine third-law partner" widget="match" config={nlPartnersMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a book resting on a table is <em>not</em> an
  action–reaction pair between the book's weight and the table's upward push. Those two forces
  both act on the <em>same</em> object (the book) and cancel there — that is an equilibrium
  statement, first-law thinking. The genuine third-law partners are: Earth pulls the book down /
  the book pulls Earth up; and the table pushes the book up / the book pushes the table down.
  Keeping "same object = balance, different objects = third law" straight will save you on every
  exam question of this type.</p>

  <h2>Turning forces: moments and centre of gravity</h2>
  <p>
    Forces do not only shove — they turn. The turning effect of a force, called its
    <strong>moment</strong>, is the force multiplied by the perpendicular distance from the pivot
    to the line of action of the force, measured in newton-metres (N·m). This is why door handles
    are placed as far from the hinges as the designer can manage: pushing with 20 N at 0.8 m from
    the hinge turns the door with a moment of 16 N·m, while the same 20 N applied 0.1 m from the
    hinge manages only 2 N·m. It is also the entire theory of levers: a wheelbarrow lets a gardener
    lift a 900 N load because the handles are five times further from the wheel (the pivot) than
    the load is, so a 180 N lift does the job. When a body is balanced — a seesaw, a bridge, a
    mobile hanging from the ceiling — the total clockwise moment about any pivot equals the total
    anticlockwise moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked seesaw: a 30 kg child (weight about 294 N) sits 2.0 m from the pivot, giving a
    moment of 588 N·m. A 20 kg child (weight about 196 N) must sit where 196 × d = 588, so
    d = 3.0 m on the other side. Light child, long arm; heavy child, short arm — the trade between
    force and distance is the whole point of a lever.
  </p>
  <p>
    The weight of an extended object — a hammer, a ladder, your own body — can be treated as a
    single force acting at one point, the <strong>centre of gravity</strong>. Its position decides
    stability. If you tilt an object and the vertical line from its centre of gravity still falls
    inside the base, the object swings back: stable. If the line falls outside the base, the
    moment of its weight now turns it further over: it topples. This is why racing cars are built
    low and wide (lower centre of gravity, wider base, harder to tip in a corner), why
    double-decker buses pass dramatic tilt tests with the top deck empty of passengers, and why
    standing on one foot makes you wobble — you have halved your base.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — stability, tops and bases" widget="judge" config={nlStabilityJudge} />

  <h2>Momentum: mass in motion</h2>
  <p>
    The second law has a deeper form. Define <strong>momentum</strong> as mass times velocity:
    <code>p = m·v</code>, measured in kg·m/s — "quantity of motion". Newton's original statement
    of the second law was that force equals the <em>rate of change of momentum</em>: <code>F =
    Δp / Δt</code>. Rearranged, <code>F·Δt = Δp</code>: the same momentum change can be bought
    with a big force for a short time or a small force for a long time. That trade is called
    impulse, and it is why cars have crumple zones and airbags. A 1,000 kg car at 20 m/s carries
    20,000 kg·m/s of momentum that must reach zero in a crash. Hitting a rigid wall in 0.1 s means
    an average force of 200,000 N; a crumpling front end that stretches the stop to 2 s cuts it to
    10,000 N — twenty times gentler on the occupants, for the same total momentum change.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the faulty line — the crumple-zone calculation" widget="spot-error" config={nlCrumpleSpot} />
  <p>
    Momentum also obeys a conservation law: with no resultant external force, the total momentum
    of a system never changes. Fire a 0.02 kg bullet from a 4 kg rifle at 400 m/s, and the
    backward momentum of the rifle must equal the forward momentum of the bullet: 8 kg·m/s, so the
    rifle kicks back at 2 m/s. This conservation idea scales all the way up — it is the bookkeeping
    behind every collision, explosion and rocket burn, and it gets a full treatment in the
    momentum lesson later in this unit.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A 1,200 kg car has 3,000 N of engine thrust and 600 N of total resistance. What is its acceleration?</li>
    <li>Why does a rocket work in the vacuum of space, where there is nothing to push against?</li>
    <li>A book rests on a table. Which pairs of forces are the third-law pairs — and why is it not weight versus the table's upward push?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Resultant = 3,000 − 600 = 2,400 N forward, so a = 2,400 ÷
    1,200 = 2 m/s² — draw the forces, find the resultant along the direction of motion,
    divide by the mass. (2) Third-law pairs act on different objects: the engine hurls
    exhaust gases backward and the gases push the rocket forward — no ground or air needed.
    (3) The genuine pairs are Earth pulls the book down / the book pulls Earth up, and the
    table pushes the book up / the book pushes the table down. Weight and the table's push
    both act on the <em>same</em> object (the book), so they balance there — first-law
    equilibrium, not a third-law pair.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Forces are vectors; only the resultant decides how motion changes.</li>
    <li>First law: no resultant force, no change in velocity — inertia is why you lurch when a bus brakes.</li>
    <li>Second law: F = m·a — force causes change in velocity, never velocity itself.</li>
    <li>Third law: equal-and-opposite pairs act on different objects, so they never cancel.</li>
    <li>Mass (kg, the measure of inertia) and weight (N, the gravitational force W = m·g) are different quantities with different instruments.</li>
    <li>Moment = force × perpendicular distance; balance means clockwise = anticlockwise moments, and stability lives at the centre of gravity.</li>
    <li>Momentum p = m·v is conserved without a resultant external force; impulse F·Δt = Δp is why crumple zones and airbags save lives.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The momentum lesson later in this unit gives conservation its full treatment. Friction's
    static-versus-kinetic subtleties, the laws' relativistic and quantum limits, and the
    eerie equivalence of inertial and gravitational mass — Einstein's road to gravity as
    curved spacetime — wait in "Going deeper" below.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The two roles mass plays — inertia in <code>F = m·a</code> and "charge" of gravity in
    <code>W = m·g</code> — are experimentally the same number to better than one part in a
    trillion. Newton had no explanation for this eerie coincidence; it became the founding
    postulate of Einstein's general relativity (the <em>equivalence principle</em>), which
    recasts gravity not as a force but as the curvature of spacetime. That is why free fall feels
    like weightlessness: in the orbiting station, gravity is doing all the accelerating, so no
    <em>extra</em> force is needed and nothing is squeezed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Friction deserves its own honest paragraph. <em>Static</em> friction (before sliding starts)
    adjusts itself up to a maximum, and that maximum is usually larger than <em>kinetic</em>
    friction (once sliding) — which is why a heavy crate takes more force to start than to keep
    moving, and why anti-lock brakes, which keep tyres gripping rather than skidding, stop a car
    in a shorter distance. The proportionality constant between friction force and the normal
    force, the coefficient of friction μ, is a property of the pair of surfaces, and ranges from
    about 0.04 for ice on steel to above 1 for rubber on dry tarmac.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the laws' limits. Newtonian mechanics fails when speeds approach the speed of light
    (special relativity replaces momentum with <code>p = m·v / √(1 − v²/c²)</code>) and at atomic
    scales (quantum mechanics). But its empire is vast: from footballs to spacecraft it is
    accurate to absurd precision, and every one of its predictions in this lesson — headrests,
    seesaws, crumple zones, rocket kicks — can be checked with a stopwatch and a set of scales.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Newton, Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica (1687); Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus (2026–2028), sections 1.3, 1.5 and 1.6; OpenStax, <em>College Physics</em>, chapters 4–5 and 8. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
